II. RELATED WORK
Recent advancements have significantly addressed PD misdiagnosis by focusing on disease-specific feature extraction and multimodal differentiation [23, 22, 14, 24, 20]. Varghese et al.[17] addressed this challenge by capturing PD-specific motor patterns, combined with non-motor symptom questionnaires, achieving 72.42% accuracy in distinguishing PD from differential diagnoses (DD) such as essential tremor and atypical parkinsonism. To address the scarcity of labeled data, self-supervised learning (SSL) has emerged as a promising alternative. SSL generates supervisory signals from the data itself, eliminating the need for expert-labeled annotations. SSL methods can be broadly categorized into context-based, contrastive, and generative approaches [5], with recent hybrid techniques combining these strategies for enhanced feature learning [13, 15]. Anomaly detection has also been explored as a complementary approach for PD diagnosis. This technique identifies instances that significantly deviate from normal patterns and is widely used in fraud detection and medical diagnosis. For time-series data, anomalies may occur at specific points, over intervals, or throughout entire sequences [9]. This paper focuses on time-series anomalies. Jiang et al. [7] formulated PD diagnosis as an anomaly detection problem, assuming PD subjects generate samples that deviate from the population mean. They employed SSL techniques to learn sensor signal representations, simulating physiological patterns of healthy subjects for anomaly detection. Despite these advancements, existing SSL approaches do not adequately address the complexities of PD diagnosis in free-living settings, particularly in distinguishing PD from similar movement disorders. Our work builds on these foundations by integrating SSL with anomaly detection to enhance PD diagnosis in this environment.
