B. Overview
Labeling activity signals in a free-living environment is both costly and subjective, making effective use of unlabeled data a key challenge for PD diagnosis. To address this, we propose a self-supervised learning framework that enhances feature extraction, particularly for distinguishing between normal and abnormal activity patterns. Our framework consists of two core modules: selfsupervised learning and anomaly detection, as illustrated in Figure 1. The objective is to leverage masked data to predict original activity signals, thereby extracting meaningful features for anomaly detection in PD diagnosis. We adopt the Transformer [18] as the backbone architecture due to its effectiveness in capturing long-range dependencies, which has been widely demonstrated in masked learning research. The encoder employs self-attention mechanisms to learn complex temporal features within the sequential data. The learned representations are then used for global feature extraction via a multi-class contractive classifier and local feature learning via a masked decoder. Specifically, the decoder
reconstructs the original input x′ from the latent representation z, facilitating local feature extraction. The encoding and decoding processes are defined as:
