The overall workflow consists of three key steps: Input Data Masking: Raw sensor signals undergo six predefined transformations. A subset of both original and transformed signals is randomly masked by replacing selected values with zeros. This step enables the model to learn robust feature representations by reconstructing the missing information. Contrastive Learning: The contrastive module employs a multi-class classifier to categorize encoder-generated features, ensuring effective global feature representation. This step is crucial for distinguishing normal and abnormal activity patterns, supporting downstream anomaly detection. Masked Learning: The masked module reconstructs the input signals using a convolutional decoder, enhancing local feature representations. This process ensures the model learns detailed signal structures, improving reconstruction accuracy.
