C. Self-Supervised Learning
1) Contrastive Learning: Contrastive learning focuses on global feature extraction by comparing different transformations of the data. In real-world environments, activity signals are often contaminated by noise (e.g., sensor detachment, power loss), which can obscure local features. To mitigate this issue, we incorporate contrastive learning, leveraging the assumption from Jing et al. [8] that normal and anomalous data remain distinguishable under consistent augmentation strategies. We apply six transformations inspired by [16], including noise addition, reverse, permutation, scaling, negation, and smoothing. Each feature dimension’s mean is computed, followed by classification using a fully connected (FC) layer composed of three MLP layers. Each MLP layer includes Batch Normalization, ReLU activation, and Dropout function (dropout rate = 0.1). The contrastive learning objective is defined by the crossentropy (CE) loss:
