B. Comparison Methods and Implementation Setup
We compared the proposed method with recent advanced models for time-series classification tasks, including FC STGNN [21], CRT[27], ts2vec[25], TimesURL[11], Channel Masking[19], and TimeMAE[3]. In our implementation, we first conducted data preprocessing, which included normalization of the raw data, sliding window partitioning, and transformation. We used the Adam optimizer for 200 epochs, setting the learning rate to 0.001. We used the reconstruction error on the validation set as the model selection criterion, and the primary evaluation metrics were accuracy, F1-score, and AUC. For each dataset, we performed subject-level dataset splitting, allocating 80% of subjects for training and 20% for testing. All samples in the training phase were from healthy subjects during training, ensuring that the model learned distinguishing features without prior exposure to abnormal cases. Table I presents the optimal performance of our proposed method compared to baseline approaches. The experimental results demonstrate that our approach outperforms all competing methods in PD diagnosis. Specifically, our method improved the accuracy by 3% on the HC vs PD dataset, by 6% on the PL vs PD dataset, by 12% on the HC+PL vs PD dataset, and by 7% on the mPower dataset. A particularly challenging classification task is HC+PL vs. PD, where PL subjects were grouped as part of the normal class. This setting increased the classification difficulty for all methods, as the model had to distinguish between PD and other conditions with similar symptoms, such as essential tremor or mild motor impairment. The added complexity in this task significantly raised the performance requirements for all classification methods. Traditional methods are often constrained by feature extraction techniques and data dimensionality. The supervised learning methods used in our experiments did not apply the most optimal feature extraction, which likely contributed to their relatively weaker performance compared to similar methods used in other studies. Given the reality of large numbers of unlabeled training instances and limited labeled data in real-world scenarios[5], our approach has significant room for further improvement. As shown in the results, the performance of all the compared methods decreases with the
