C. Masking Ratio
Fig. 2: Classification performance changes with different time masking ratios. As we can see, a high masking ratio (90%) achieves optimal performance in discriminating between HC and PD patients.
The masking ratio determines the proportion of useful information in the input data, directly impacting the difficulty of the reconstruction task. To investigate this, we fixed the random seed and other model parameters and evaluated the effect of the masking ratio on the performance of the selfsupervised model using the collected dataset. The results are shown in Table 2. Our findings indicate that a low masking ratio fails to introduce enough variation in the output. As the masking ratio increases, the most effective ratio was found to be 90%. This result aligns with the findings of Wang et al. [improved], and is higher than the optimal masking ratio identified by He et al. in the image domain [6]. The data collected from subjects are influenced by individual differences and contain more noise, particularly in unconstrained environments. Therefore, the model requires a higher masking ratio to generate distinct yet reasonable outputs. This experiment demonstrates that mask-based self-supervised learning is highly effective for large-scale wearable sensor signal data and is a valuable tool for early diagnosis of PD.
