To validate the independent contributions and synergistic effects of the modules in our framework, we conducted an ablation study. In the ablation study, we designed three comparative model configurations: 1) Full Model: Includes both the masked learning and contrastive learning modules. 2) Without Contrastive Learning: Removes the contrastive learning module. 3) Without Masked Learning: Removes the masked learning module.
4) Without Contrastive Learning and Masked Learning: Removes the contrastive learning and masked learning modules, retaining only the fully connected module. The experimental results are shown in Table II. The results show a clear complementarity between masked learning and contrastive learning within the framework. Masked learning, as the foundational module, plays a decisive role in extracting features from time-series data, while contrastive learning enhances model performance by further optimizing the discriminative power of the features. The greater performance degradation when masked learning is removed validates its central role in the framework.
V. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK
In this paper, we propose a novel self-supervised learning framework that integrates masked learning and contrastive learning for PD diagnosis using wearable sensor data collected in unconstrained environments. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first application of a masking mechanism for PD diagnosis within a self-supervised learning paradigm. Our approach is designed to effectively leverage large-scale unlabeled data by learning normal activity patterns from healthy subjects, while the incorporation of contrastive learning further enhances feature discrimination, leading to improved anomaly detection performance. To validate the effectiveness of our method, we conducted comprehensive evaluations on publicly available datasets. The experimental results demonstrate that our approach consistently outperforms traditional unsupervised and supervised methods, exhibiting superior accuracy, robustness, and generalization. Notably, our framework excels in scenarios with limited labeled data, highlighting its potential for real-world clinical applications. While our current study focuses on binary classification for PD detection, future research will extend the proposed masked-contrastive self-supervised learning framework to PD severity classification tasks. Additionally, we plan to incorporate pre-trained self-supervised models from other domains to further enhance feature representation learning. Finally, we will investigate the integration of domain adaptation techniques to improve model robustness across different datasets and sensor configurations.
